  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nSouthern California will be in a push-and-pull for the remainder \nof the week between a deepening low over the PacNW, high pressure \nover Texas, and two tropical cyclones in the eastern Pacific. A \nweak ridge will hold through at least Friday and keep temperatures\nwell above normal with highs in the 90s for inland and High \nDesert, 80s for the coast and mountains, and 100-103 for the low \ndeserts. Things start to change this weekend as the ridge is \ncompressed and the high over Texas shifts southward, allowing for \ntropical moisture to shift up Baja and into Southern California \nand western Arizona. This could mean some very warm morning \ntemperatures both Saturday and Sunday as the moisture reduces \nradiational cooling, and temperatures in the afternoon remain \nelevated as heights will be slow to fall. This is likely to bring \nabout some Moderate HeatRisk for the coastal areas on Saturday \nwith temperatures around 10-12 degrees above normal and warm \novernight lows above 71 degrees. A lot of this will depend heavily\non cloud cover associated with the tropical cyclones well to our \nsouth, which could impact peak heating on Saturday, limiting our \nwarming.\n\nBy late weekend, a trough off the aforementioned larger low in the \nPacNW will swing down and become a closed low by Monday. This could \nhelp initiate some convection while mixing with the influx of \nmoisture. For now, much of any precipitation looks to stay east of \nthe region, as drier air on the backside of the trough looks to push \nin as tropical moisture is shunted east of the mountains. Should \nSoCal get dry slotted (80-90% confidence), Sunday and Monday would at \nleast feature cooler weather thanks to the deepening low as it \nmeanders into Arizona and Utah. Meanwhile, other states in the \nDesert SW may experience multiple rounds of precipitation and some \nheavy rainfall. At this point, the tracks of Polo and Odalys and \nwhere the larger upper level pattern will be are not conducive to \nprecipitation for SoCal. \n\nWith building high pressure to the west and drier air filter in, the \nregion will return to a warming trend after Monday along with \nquieter weather.\n\nA few impacts that may be observed early next week due to the \ntropical cyclones: \n-higher humidity for the deserts, especially the low deserts, late \nweekend into early next week. \n-coastal flooding and coastal erosion, especially for south-facing \nbeaches, Sunday through Tuesday. Additional details on this can be \nfound in the Beaches discussion below.",
